##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    4249 1451  166
                                                                  4249 1451  167
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       4249 1451  168
                                                                  4249 1451  169
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          4249 1451  170
                                                                  4249 1451  171
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   4249 1451  172
                                                                  4249 1451  173
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 4249 1451  174
                                                                  4249 1451  175
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     4249 1451  176
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   4249 1451  177
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             4249 1451  178
                                                                  4249 1451  179
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                4249 1451  180
                                                                  4249 1451  181
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       4249 1451  182
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  4249 1451  183
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   4249 1451  184
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  4249 1451  185
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  4249 1451  186
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 4249 1451  187
to the highest incident energy.                                   4249 1451  188
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:4249 1451  189
                                                                  4249 1451  190
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  4249 1451  191
                                                                  4249 1451  192
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    4249 1451  193
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     4249 1451  194
                                                                  4249 1451  195
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     4249 1451  196
                                                                  4249 1451  197
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    4249 1451  198
total cross section.                                              4249 1451  199
                                                                  4249 1451  200
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            4249 1451  201
                                                                  4249 1451  202
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    4249 1451  203
                                                                  4249 1451  204
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        4249 1451  205
                                                                  4249 1451  206
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  4249 1451  207
                                                                  4249 1451  208
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           4249 1451  209
                                                                  4249 1451  210
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   4249 1451  211
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        4249 1451  212
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     4249 1451  213
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         4249 1451  214
sections.                                                         4249 1451  215
                                                                  4249 1451  216
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 4249 1451  217
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 4249 1451  218
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 4249 1451  219
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 4249 1451  220
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      4249 1451  221
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       4249 1451  222
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              4249 1451  223
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  4249 1451  224
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  4249 1451  225
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  4249 1451  226
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  4249 1451  227
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  4249 1451  228
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       4249 1451  229
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        4249 1451  230
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4249 1451  231
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        4249 1451  232
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4249 1451  233
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        4249 1451  234
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4249 1451  235
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        4249 1451  236
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       4249 1451  237
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        4249 1451  238
                                                                  4249 1451  239
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           4249 1451  240
                                                                  4249 1451  241
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    4249 1451  242
discrete levels.                                                  4249 1451  243
                                                                  4249 1451  244
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         4249 1451  245
                                                                  4249 1451  246
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  4249 1451  247
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   4249 1451  248
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      4249 1451  249
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  4249 1451  250
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      4249 1451  251
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         4249 1451  252
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   4249 1451  253
on an angular grid.                                               4249 1451  254
                                                                  4249 1451  255
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       4249 1451  256
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        4249 1451  257
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4249 1451  258
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4249 1451  259
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4249 1451  260
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        4249 1451  261
                                                                  4249 1451  262
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     4249 1451  263
                                                                  4249 1451  264
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     4249 1451  265
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   4249 1451  266
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  4249 1451  267
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             4249 1451  268
All data are generated with TALYS.                                4249 1451  269
                                                                  4249 1451  270
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   4249 1451  271
                                                                  4249 1451  272
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      4249 1451  273
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   4249 1451  274
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   4249 1451  275
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are4249 1451  276
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   4249 1451  277
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  4249 1451  278
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              4249 1451  279
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 4249 1451  280
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  4249 1451  281
For each residual product:                                        4249 1451  282
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               4249 1451  283
- the recoil                                                      4249 1451  284
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         4249 1451  285
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     4249 1451  286
sections.                                                         4249 1451  287
                                                                  4249 1451  288
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     4249 1451  289
                                                                  4249 1451  290
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  4249 1451  291
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 4249 1451  292
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          4249 1451  293
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       4249 1451  294
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 4249 1451  295
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     4249 1451  296
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  4249 1451  297
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      4249 1451  298
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         4249 1451  299
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             4249 1451  300
                                                                  4249 1451  301
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   4249 1451  302
followed:                                                         4249 1451  303
                                                                  4249 1451  304
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4249 1451  305
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4249 1451  306
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4249 1451  307
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     4249 1451  308
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4249 1451  309
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4249 1451  310
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4249 1451  311
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4249 1451  312
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4249 1451  313
                                                                  4249 1451  314
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 4249 1451  315
                                                                  4249 1451  316
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    4249 1451  317
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          4249 1451  318
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     4249 1451  319
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          4249 1451  320
                                                                  4249 1451  321
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  4249 1451  322
                                                                  4249 1451  323
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     4249 1451  324
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    4249 1451  325
as isomer.                                                        4249 1451  326
                                                                  4249 1451  327
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 4249 1451  328
                                                                  4249 1451  329
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        4249 1451  330
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    4249 1451  331
as isomer.                                                        4249 1451  332
                                                                  4249 1451  333
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          4249 1451  334
                                                                  4249 1451  335
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:4249 1451  336
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   4249 1451  337
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   4249 1451  338
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   4249 1451  339
  given                                                           4249 1451  340
                                                                  4249 1451  341
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          4249 1451  342
                                                                  4249 1451  343
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  4249 1451  344
                                                                  4249 1451  345
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 4249 1451  346
                                                                  4249 1451  347
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         4249 1451  348
                                                                  4249 1451  349
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   4249 1451  350
                                                                  4249 1451  351
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        4249 1451  352
and more details are given above in this description. In most     4249 1451  353
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        4249 1451  354
                                                                  4249 1451  355
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 4249 1451  356
                                                                  4249 1451  357
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  4249 1451  358
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     4249 1451  359
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    4249 1451  360
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   4249 1451  361
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  4249 1451  362
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                4249 1451  363
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      4249 1451  364
   same reaction channel between different energies)              4249 1451  365
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      4249 1451  366
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               4249 1451  367
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      4249 1451  368
   different reaction channels for different energies)            4249 1451  369
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        4249 1451  370
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              4249 1451  371
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  4249 1451  372
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       4249 1451  373
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     4249 1451  374
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  4249 1451  375
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     4249 1451  376
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     4249 1451  377
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     4249 1451  378
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   4249 1451  379
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   4249 1451  380
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  4249 1451  381
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   4249 1451  382
                                                                  4249 1451  383
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  4249 1451  384
                                                                  4249 1451  385
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       4249 1451  386
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  4249 1451  387
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       4249 1451  388
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   4249 1451  389
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         4249 1451  390
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  4249 1451  391
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      4249 1451  392
representation is used.                                           4249 1451  393
                                                                  4249 1451  394
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      4249 1451  395
                                                                  4249 1451  396
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  4249 1451  397
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             4249 1451  398
                                                                  4249 1451  399
